= Brief History
In Ancient Times:
+ Atheneaus of Naucratis (birth-death years) wrote that the Ancient Greeks had a custom in which 
kids dress as birds and go door-to-door singing songs
+ If the owner of a house did not give them food, they would cause mischief

16th Century Scotland:
+ Guising
+ Etymology: what does this word mean? 
+ Definition:  tradition in which children go door-to-door in disguise and receive gifts
+ Can be traced to Celtic festival of _Samhain_, in which people impersonated spirits as protection from them, received gifts in exchange

Trick-or-treating:
+ Guising spreads to North America first in Canada (1911), later to Chicago (1920), and subsequently the rest of the U.S.
+ The practice of getting food from strangers, called Ragamuffin Day, had existed since 1870 but was commonly done on Thanksgiving
+ Trick-or-treating became more and more popular, with a brief pause due to rationing during WWII 

